  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\u27e8x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=\u27e8x,\\,\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9,\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
